 ],
 "literal": "Let them say many things, those who do not know that! / The waterfall-brought fresh shoot of eruvai grass, / the pool-seeking elephant eats as a mouthful, / the friendship of the lord of the mountain-possessing land, / that it will not reach an end, I knew very well indeed.",
 "translation": "Let those who do not understand say whatever they want! I know very well that my intimacy with the lord of the mountain country—where elephants seeking mountain pools feed on mouthfuls of fresh eruvai grass-shoots brought down by the waterfalls—will never come to an end.",
 "modern": "அதை அறியாதவர்கள் பலவற்றையும் சொல்லட்டும். அருவி கொண்டுவந்த புதிய எருவைப் புல்லின் தளிர்களை, சுனையை நாடிச் செல்லும் யானை கவளமாக உண்ணும் மலைகள் நிறைந்த நாட்டின் தலைவனுடைய நட்பு, ஒருபோதும் அழியாது என்பதை நான் நன்றாகவே அறிந்துள்ளேன்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her companion, expressing her absolute confidence in her relationship with the hero. She dismisses the gossip of those who do not understand their bond, telling them to say whatever they please. The imagery belongs to the Kuriñci (mountainous) landscape, featuring waterfalls, mountain pools, elephants, and eruvai grass. The elephant seeking the pool and eating the fresh grass brought by the waterfall symbolizes how the hero naturally seeks her out and derives sustenance and joy from their relationship. The key words are 'கேண்மை' (intimacy/friendship) and 'தலைபோகாமை' (not ending/not dying out), which emphasize the eternal nature of their love.",
